# FOMO unattended operation -- crontab fragment (Phase 36, SCHED-08, D-01/D-04/D-18).
#
# Copy this fragment into the FOMO service account's crontab (`crontab -e`) and replace
# BOTH placeholder paths below with the real absolute paths on this host:
#   /path/to/venv/bin/python      -- the virtualenv's python interpreter
#   /path/to/checkout/manage.py   -- this checkout's manage.py
#
# This file is committed to git -- it must NEVER gain a real secret, a real host path, or
# any other host-specific value. `python manage.py check_unattended` (a later plan in this
# phase) prints this same line with the real resolved interpreter and manage.py paths
# already filled in, so you do not have to hand-edit the placeholders from scratch.
#
# The environment the cron daemon must supply (by NAME only -- never a value here, D-15):
#   FOMO_HEARTBEAT_URL  -- a healthchecks.io-compatible ping URL (optional; unset disables
#                          the heartbeat layer, D-12)
#   FOMO_BASE_URL       -- used to build the admin/calendar links in a failure email. WR-13
#                          (36-REVIEW.md): the campaign-submission approval-queue link is
#                          built inside the WEB process (gunicorn/uWSGI), not this cron
#                          process, so FOMO_BASE_URL must be set in BOTH environments --
#                          or, simpler, set once in this host's local_settings.py so every
#                          process picks up the same value.
# The LCO/SOAR API key and the mail (EMAIL_HOST_*) credentials belong in
# `fomo/local_settings.py` on this host, never in this file and never in the process
# argument vector, where a local `ps aux` would expose them (threat T-36-02).
#
# The schedule: every 15 minutes (D-04), guarded by a non-blocking flock so a slow tick
# never overlaps the next one. `flock -n -E 99` fails immediately (rather than queuing)
# with exit code 99 if the lock is already held, so a permanently stuck tick would
# otherwise look like a healthy no-op -- the `[ $? -eq 99 ] && echo ... skipped` tail
# below leaves a visible trail in the log for exactly that case, and the runner's own
# heartbeat (D-12) is the structural backstop if this log line is never checked --
# but only if the check at the other end is configured with BOTH its expected ping
# interval (15 min, matching this schedule; healthchecks.io calls this Period) and
# its grace time (about 20 min). See the runbook's "How do I run everything
# unattended?" section for the full setup. WR-01 (36-REVIEW.md): the tail is gated
# on flock's own dedicated exit code 99, not on "any non-zero exit" --
# `run_unattended` itself exits 1 whenever a step failed, and a plain `||` tail
# would mislabel that failing-but-genuinely-ran tick as "lock held" in the log.
#
# The lock file here (`run_unattended.cron.lock`) is deliberately NOT the same file
# `run_unattended` itself locks internally (`run_unattended.lock`, via `command_lock()`
# in unattended.py) -- `flock(2)` locks are per open file description, so a child
# process's own attempt to lock the same path its parent's `flock -n` already holds
# would be denied by its own parent, silently turning every scheduled tick into a no-op
# (CR-01, 36-REVIEW.md). Keep these two lock file names distinct.
#
# WR-09 (36-REVIEW.md): the line captures flock's own exit code into `$rc` right away and
# ends with an explicit `exit $rc`, so the line's own reported status is always
# `run_unattended`'s (0 healthy, 1 failing, 99 lock held) -- the earlier `[ $? -eq 99 ] &&
# echo ...` tail was the line's *last* command, so cron saw the tail's own exit status
# (1 unless the tail actually fired) instead of the tick's.
*/15 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n -E 99 /var/lock/fomo/run_unattended.cron.lock /path/to/venv/bin/python /path/to/checkout/manage.py run_unattended >> /var/log/fomo/unattended.log 2>&1; rc=$?; [ $rc -eq 99 ] && echo "$(date -Is) run_unattended skipped: lock held" >> /var/log/fomo/unattended.log; exit $rc

# Before installing this line on a fresh host, create the two directories the schedule
# above assumes exist and are writable by the cron user:
#   /var/lock/fomo
#   /var/log/fomo
# See the "How do I run everything unattended?" runbook section
# (docs/runbooks/telescope_runs_calendar.rst) for the full setup walkthrough, and
# deploy/logrotate/fomo.example (a later plan in this phase) for rotating the log file.
